Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action drama, beginning in late-1990s Milwaukee and ending eight years later.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A fifth-floor walk-up in a Milwaukee brick building. OLIVIA “MA” OLSON, mid-60s, frail, brown-haired and poorly sighted, dozes beneath an afghan.

THE NARRATOR, her soft-spoken younger son in his late 20s, watches a game show beside her.

The door opens. NICK OLSON, early 30s, huge and broad-shouldered, enters in heavy boots and a brown suede jacket. His reddish-brown hair is uncombed. Scars cross his chin and left eyebrow.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. His face looked like a puzzle with the pieces held together with glue. When he walked in, people took notice, and man, did they move.

Nick looks toward the bedroom.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don’t know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill.

The Narrator lowers his eyes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Dad took off when I was eight. After that, Nick became my dad. He would stand up for me no matter what. He also made sure I never forgot who was in charge.

INT. MOXIE’S GYM - DAY

A weightlifter abandons a curl bench the instant Nick approaches. Nick replaces the forty-pound bar with a fifty and begins effortless curls.

The Narrator watches with pride and unease.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was a modern-day warrior. A real-life Viking. Strong, unwavering, and free to do whatever he wanted.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Medicine bottles stand in a careful line. Ma searches among them, increasingly distressed.

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR
Ma, we haven’t been to church for fifteen years. Your pills are right in front of you.

Ma stares through him with empty, glassy eyes.

MA
Oh. Right. I am so sorry. I don’t know what got into me.

INT. JOE’S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

A Packers game plays above a smoky bar. Nick’s scarred hand lands heavily on the Narrator’s shoulder.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

THE NARRATOR
Ma got confused. She didn’t know where her pills were. She asked if we went to church.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Why don’t you take her to the store or something? Do something before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

THE NARRATOR
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

Nick’s black eyes harden. His fist tightens around his glass.

NICK
Get home and watch Ma.

EXT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

The Narrator leaves the bar, then stops beneath the library’s glowing windows.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old lamps hang over endless shelves. The Narrator receives a temporary card, then wanders into classic literature.

He takes WUTHERING HEIGHTS from the shelf and holds it like contraband.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I liked reading. I didn’t tell nobody that. Who needed school when everything was right here for free?

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

The Narrator parks his bicycle. SIONNA MCALLISTER, late 20s, slender and pale, trips on the pavement and brushes his shoulder.

Her short brown hair frames extraordinary light-blue eyes. She wears jeans, blue tennis shoes, a black V-neck shirt and a necklace bearing a fish symbol.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. They stare at each other.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? What does that mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me after an Irish goddess associated with the River Shannon.

THE NARRATOR
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number and hands it to him.

As Sionna walks away, she looks back over her shoulder.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Her eyes were the blue of the sky right before sunrise. She was intense, like the tide coming in, and I didn’t want to move my feet.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students crowd a fashionable coffee shop. Sionna sits opposite the Narrator, now holding his battered copy of WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
The environment is like its people — wild and unpredictable. Heathcliff’s love for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him. A love that never changes will destroy you.

The Narrator absorbs this.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

The Narrator struggles on a purple mat while Sionna moves gracefully beside him. His shirt falls over his face during downward dog.

Later, both lie still in corpse pose.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

THE NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

THE NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

They smile.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

A blue weekly pill container rests beside Ma. The Narrator fills its compartments while Sionna checks the bottles.

Nick storms in.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR
I met a girl. We had a couple dates.

NICK
A girl? Don’t you think Ma deserves better than that? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

THE NARRATOR
I said I was sorry!

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick! You haven’t even met her.

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
You’re so Goddamn hateful. What got into you?

Nick SLAPS the Narrator across the head. His left ear rings.

NICK
Don’t you ever talk that way about my mother.

Nick slams the door behind him.

The Narrator steadies himself against the table. Ma watches from her chair, frightened and confused.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had never stood up to Nick before. It came bubbling out like a box of firecrackers exploding from my mouth.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - DAY

The Narrator and Sionna walk past laughing teenagers.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like I want to punch that kid in the face. Doesn’t that piss you off?

SIONNA
No. Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

They stop beneath a tree.

SIONNA
Loving kindness means opening our hearts to everyone, even people we don’t particularly like.

THE NARRATOR
That’s going to be hard.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

The Narrator stares at her, overwhelmed.

THE NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

They kiss.

INT. JOE’S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Nick sees his former girlfriend LINDSEY beside her new companion, MARCO. Nick shoves through the poolroom crowd.

NICK
What’s up, Lindsey? Who is this?

LINDSEY
An old friend.

MARCO puts a protective arm around her.

MARCO
What’s your problem? I’ve never even seen you before, and you’re in my face.

Nick draws back his fist.

The Narrator steps between them.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you don’t have any problem with this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick swings. The Narrator jerks backward as JOE and BOUNCER FRANK seize Nick.

Nick head-butts Frank. Lindsey pulls Marco toward the exit.

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

The Narrator runs.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

The Narrator lies with his head in Sionna’s lap. She strokes his hair.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
He is controlling, and you are showing him that you don’t need him.

THE NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

THE NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Sionna serves Ma warm peach pie. Ma laughs, animated and happy.

The door opens. Nick enters and reluctantly shakes Sionna’s hand.

SIONNA
You must be Nick. I’ve heard so much about you.

NICK
Likewise.

NICK eyes her as she sits beside Ma.

NICK
I hear you’re a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I’m thinking of becoming a college professor.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off. Especially for a woman.

SIONNA
There are more women in college today than men. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

Nick has no answer. Sionna carries the empty plates into the kitchen.

Nick grips the Narrator’s shirt and pulls him close.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
You know I will.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment is silent. Ma’s chair is empty. Her purse and coat are gone.

Five messages blink on the answering machine.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER MIKE DUPUIS, 50s, graying and solemn, sits across from the Narrator.

OFFICER DUPUIS
There has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother. They were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe.

The Narrator grips a paper cup.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

The cup crumples in the Narrator’s shaking hand.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Five days later. Nick lies bruised and diminished beneath an oxygen tube, his right leg immobilized.

NICK
Hey, Bro. I guess I really fucked up. I can’t believe Ma’s gone.

THE NARRATOR
The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK
I had three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church. They’re saying vehicular manslaughter. It was a mistake. I was almost killed too.

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

The Narrator looks at his helpless brother and finally sees no giant, no Viking — only a frightened man trapped among tubes.

THE NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He walks out.

EXT. ST. MARY’S CEMETERY - DAY

A handful of earth strikes Ma’s light maple casket.

Later, a stone bears her name: OLIVIA ANNE OLSON.

The Narrator places golden mums beneath it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I lost my mother. It seemed I had also lost my brother.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Sionna opens the door. Nick forces his way inside, gaunt from his injuries but still physically imposing.

NICK
You’re the reason I’m going to jail. You confused his stupid brain with school and all that other shit.

SIONNA
This has nothing to do with me. Sit down and tell me what happened.

NICK
You get away from my brother.

Nick drives her against the hallway wall and clamps one hand around her throat.

Sionna claws at his wrist, choking.

Nick flicks open a knife and presses it below her left collarbone.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. If you tell the cops or anyone else, I’ll come back.

The front door BANGS open. The Narrator freezes at the sight.

THE NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick! What are you doing?

He grabs Nick’s arm.

Nick jerks free. The knife plunges into Sionna’s upper chest.

Blood spreads across her shirt.

Sionna sinks toward the floor. Nick flees.

The Narrator catches her and tears away the blood-soaked fabric. He presses both hands over the wound.

THE NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay with me.

Her breathing becomes shallow.

Sirens approach.

THE NARRATOR
Hurry up. Please, hurry up.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. Sionna sits rigidly in a chair, her left arm supported after surgery. The light has gone from her blue eyes.

THE NARRATOR
You know I love you. You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

THE NARRATOR kneels before her.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. I need to go to Mom and Dad’s. I need to see a therapist.

THE NARRATOR
Please stay. We can work things out.

Sionna looks down at her hands.

The Narrator understands that she is already gone.

INT. LOWELL’S RESTAURANT - SEATTLE - DAY

Eight years later. Rain veils Puget Sound beyond the windows.

The Narrator, now a composed businessman in his late 30s, sits before an untouched cinnamon roll. A folder beside him bears the logo TONY’S BOY’S PIZZA.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I finished my degree and built a chain of pizzerias in Seattle. Nick went to prison for the stabbing and the drunk driving death of my mother. Sionna survived, but she left my life.

The frosting spirals like a pale, unblinking eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison. One night I understood that I had the key inside myself.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - VISITING ROOM - DAY

The Narrator sits behind thick plexiglass.

Nick enters in prison clothes. His hair has thinned and grayed. The former warrior looks old and small.

They lift their telephones.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
I don’t have much time, so I need you to listen.

Nick nods.

THE NARRATOR
You killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my entire life. You tried to kill the love of my life, and she left because of you.

Nick lowers his eyes.

THE NARRATOR
But this is the most important part. I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears run down Nick’s scarred face and fall onto the counter.

NICK
I’m so sorry. You’re right. I did all that shit. You’re more than I ever was. You’re a big man, and I’m a small one.

A guard signals that time is nearly over.

THE NARRATOR
I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

Nick smiles through his tears.

NICK
President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man.

The guard takes Nick away.

Nick looks back once.

NICK
Bye.

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

The Narrator approaches the gate as travelers surge around him.

The attendant scans his boarding pass. For the first time, the printed name is visible: PETER OLSON.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

Peter smiles and walks toward the plane.

THE NARRATOR
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

He pauses at the end of the jet bridge, looking toward the open sky.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
